                "The Tripartite Account Taxonomy: Nostro, Vostro, and Loro: In correspondent banking, bank accounts are classified using three classical Latin pronouns reflecting the legal perspective of the reporting bank: (1) Nostro Account ('Ours'): A bank account that a domestic bank maintains with an overseas correspondent bank, denominated in the foreign currency of the host nation. For example, Titan National Bank (Mumbai) maintains a US Dollar checking account with JPMorgan Chase Bank in New York. To Titan Bank, this account is its 'Nostro Account' ('Our money on deposit with you in USD'); (2) Vostro Account ('Yours'): A bank account maintained by a domestic bank on its own books on behalf of a foreign correspondent bank, denominated in the domestic currency. When JPMorgan Chase Bank (New York) maintains an Indian Rupee account with Titan National Bank in Mumbai, Titan records this on its general ledger as a 'Vostro Account' ('Your money on deposit with us in INR'); and (3) Loro Account ('Theirs'): A third-party reference used when two banks discuss the account of a third institution. If Titan Bank in Mumbai executes a transaction with Deutsche Bank in Frankfurt that involves funds held in an account of Barclays Bank in London, Titan refers to Barclays' account as a 'Loro Account'.",
